xmodel/nodal-realization-86-96-grok46-20260831.md:24:- Galindo conversion (Prop. 2.1, “divides” case `a|d`): `β̄_0=a=d−n` and the first transverse numerator is `β_1 = d^2/gcd(d,n) − c` when `Δ=(d,n,c)`.
xmodel/nodal-realization-86-96-grok46-20260831.md:35:Here `a=2`, `gcd(d,n)=2`. Contact 8 is a multiple of `a` strictly below the sole odd `β_1`. The complete Galindo list is `Δ=(8,6,c)` with `c` odd, `2c∈⟨8,6⟩` (i.e. `c∈⟨3,4⟩`), and `c<24`. Thus `c∈{23,21,19,17,15,13,11,9,7,3}` (the omitted odd value 5 fails `5∈⟨3,4⟩`; 1 fails the same). Conversion: `β_1=8^2/2−c=32−c`. The germ is one-pair `(2;β_1)` with `gcd(2,β_1)=1`, so `2δ_∞=β_1−1` and `δ_∞=(β_1−1)/2`. The degree-8 genus is 21, hence `δ_aff=21−δ_∞`. Cluster `(2^{δ_∞},1,1)`, `M_emb=2+β_1−1=β_1+1=M_∞` (since `β_1≥9>7`). Promoted `(M-INF)` with threshold `3d−3=21` kills the six types with `M_∞≤20` and leaves exactly the four charged survivors.
xmodel/nodal-realization-86-96-grok46-20260831.md:48:Leading form: `p^3−q^4` has `t`-degree `ν`, and `u−v^4=(p^3−q^4)/p^4` has `s`-valuation `32−ν`. After `v=r^2`, even valuations are absorbed into `ℂ[[v]]`; `β_1` is the first *odd* exponent, so `β_1=32−c` with `c` the first odd degree of a remainder of `p^3−q^4` modulo `⟨8,6⟩`. Thus `c` is an invariant of the pair `(p,q)`, not of a single quasihomogeneous polynomial.
xmodel/nodal-realization-86-96-grok46-20260831.md:52:Here `a=3`, `gcd(d,n)=3`. Contact 9 lies below the sole characteristic numerator. Complete list: `Δ=(9,6,c)` with `c<18`, `3∤c`, and `c∈⟨3,2⟩`, i.e. `c∈{17,16,14,13,11,10,8,7,5,4,2}`. Conversion: `β_1=9^2/3−c=27−c`. One-pair germ `(3;β_1)` with `gcd(3,β_1)=1`, so `δ_∞=β_1−1`. Degree-9 genus 28 gives `δ_aff=28−δ_∞`. Cluster `M(3,β_1)` is `(3^k,1^3)` for `β_1=3k+1` and `(3^k,2,1^2)` for `β_1=3k+2`; `M_emb=3+β_1−1=β_1+2=M_∞`. Threshold `3d−3=24` kills the first nine types and leaves the two charged survivors.
xmodel/nodal-realization-86-96-grok46-20260831.md:59:Leading form: `u−v^3=(p^2−q^3)/p^3` has `s`-valuation `27−ν` with `ν=deg(p^2−q^3)`, and `β_1=27−c` with `c` the first degree of a remainder of `p^2−q^3` modulo `⟨9,6⟩` that is not already in the semigroup.
xmodel/nodal-realization-86-96-grok46-20260831.md:70:**Normal form.** Kill `t^7` in `p` and, by `p↦p+δ q`, the `t^6` term of `p`. The odd condition `h_{23}(p^3−q^4)=−4a_5` (here `a_5` is the `t^5` coefficient of `q`) forces `a_5=0` already for `β_1>9`, hence for this type. Residual:
xmodel/nodal-realization-86-96-grok46-20260831.md:77:Nine coefficients. Odd coefficients of `p^3−q^4` at `t^{23},t^{21},t^{19},t^{17},t^{15},t^{13}` must vanish, and the `t^{11}` coefficient must not; even coefficients need not vanish. This is five odd conditions (after `h_{23}`) on nine parameters, expected positive-dimensional.
xmodel/nodal-realization-86-96-grok46-20260831.md:79:**Even sub-locus `b=0` (desk, not general).** Imposing also `h_{22}=h_{20}=h_{18}=h_{16}=0` (even Tschirnhausen) yields `B=4γ/3`, `C=4d/3`, `D=4e/3`, `E=(4/3)f+(2/9)γ^2`, `F=(4/9)γ d`, `G=(4/9)γ e+(2/9)d^2`. The remaining odd conditions `h_{15}=h_{13}=0` with `γ=1` (scale) reduce on `d,e,f` to `2d^3+3e^3=0` together with a quadratic over `ℚ(∛(−2/3),√73)`. At every such point the *even* coefficient `h_{12}` is the nonzero element `−217368+16168√73` of `ℚ(√73)` (conjugate likewise). That computation kills only the even-reduced slice, and only the demand that `deg(p^3−q^4)≤11` rather than “first odd remainder =11”. It is **not** a non-realisation of the type: even terms of `p^3−q^4` do not move `β_1`. The locus `γ=0` is the even-function cover `(p,q)∈ℂ[t^2]`, map degree 2, out of class.
xmodel/nodal-realization-86-96-grok46-20260831.md:85:**Verdict: OPEN.** Missing computation (CAS-scale). Ring `R=ℚ[B,C,D,E,F,G,b,γ,d,e,f]` (11 vars; one may set `f=0` by `y`-translation and `γ=1` if `γ≠0`, leaving 9). Let `H=p^3−q^4=∑_{k=0}^{23} h_k t^k`. Ideal of the type:
xmodel/nodal-realization-86-96-grok46-20260831.md:122:**Sourced lead, not a kill.** Fujimoto–Suzuki–Yokoyama, *On polynomial curves in the affine plane*, Osaka J. Math. **43** (2006), 597–608, p. 602: “Moh [11] showed that there is no polynomial curve with the `δ`-sequence `{6,8,3}`.” Their convention (Theorem 2: `δ_0<δ_1`; Fact 4: `deg_t x=deg_y f`) makes `{6,8,3}` exactly the present type after swapping coordinates (`x=q` degree 6, `y=p` degree 8, extra generator 3). They note that the *semigroup* `⟨6,8,3⟩=⟨3,8⟩` *is* realised by `(t^3,t^8)`, whose `δ`-sequence is `{3,8}`, not `{6,8,3}`. The citation is Moh, *On the Jacobian conjecture and the configurations of roots*, J. Reine Angew. Math. **340** (1983), 140–212. That PDF was not obtained (GDZ/EUDML endpoints did not yield article bytes). A secondary one-line citation, without Moh’s argument on disk, is not a sourced obstruction in the sense of this lane: it is a lead. The same FSY paper *does* give a complete Gröbner-basis non-realisation of `{6,21,4}` and `{6,22,17}` by their Lemma 1 (the second approximate root `g_2(x(t),y(t))` must have `t`-degree `δ_2`); that method applies verbatim here and is the CAS job below.
xmodel/nodal-realization-86-96-grok46-20260831.md:132:in the ring of §2, open `h_3≠0`, plus `I_{DP}` of length 7 reduced with distinct tangents. Ten odd generators, ~9 variables: overdetermined, msolve-scale; equivalently FSY Lemma 1 for `{6,8,3}`: write the second approximate root `g_2` of the monic-in-`y` defining polynomial from their Theorem 3 and impose `deg_t g_2(q(t),p(t))=3`, then saturate against `h_3=0` and the gcd-2 locus. Same positive/negative controls. If `I_29=⟨1⟩` (or every point of `V(I_29)` has non-reduced `I_{DP}`), the type is NON-REALIZABLE as a polynomial curve, hence as a nodal residual; if a nodal closed point exists it is REALIZED.
xmodel/nodal-realization-86-96-grok46-20260831.md:173:**Square-root / involution construction.** If `2∈S_aff` then some `r∈ℂ[p,q]` has degree 2. Completing the square, `r=t^2` and `q` is a polynomial in `r` of degree 6, hence even, returning to the killed locus unless `p` supplies mixed terms *and* `r` is not exactly `t^2` in the Tschirnhausen chart of `p`. The involution `τ(t)=−t−a` of a genuine quadratic `r` forces `q(t)=q(τ(t))` identically when `q∈ℂ[r]`; the equation `p(t)=p(τ(t))` is degree 9 and `τ`-invariant, hence contributes `(9−1)/2=4` unordered pairs plus the fixed point of `τ`. That is exactly `δ_aff=4` *if* those four pairs are ordinary nodes and the cubic `r↦q(r)` contributes no extra identifications with matching `p`. Extra identifications are the generic behaviour of a cubic (3-to-1). Forcing them empty is a closed condition on `p` of expected codimension equal to the number of extra pairs, not evaluated at desk. If that condition holds, this *would* be a nodal realisation; it is not exhibited.
xmodel/nodal-realization-86-96-grok46-20260831.md:195:**Approximate-root job (FSY Lemma 1).** For a putative polynomial curve with `δ`-sequence `{δ_0,δ_1,δ_2}` the second approximate root `g_2` of the monic-in-`y` equation satisfies `deg_t g_2(x(t),y(t))=δ_2`. Vanishing of all higher `t`-coefficients is a polynomial system in the parametrization coefficients. This is the exact job that FSY ran for `{6,21,4}` and `{6,22,17}` (CAS, hours, weighted LEX). It is the same finite problem as the `h_k`/`k_i` ideals of §§2–7, written in approximate-root rather than leading-form coordinates.
