xmodel/nodal-realization-86-96-grok46-20260831.md:107:**Admissibility.** `Δ=(8,6,7)`, `2·7=14=8+6∈⟨8,6⟩`, `7<24`. Gaps of `⟨8,6,7⟩`: nine, as in §1.1. Germ `(2;25)`, `δ_∞=12`, `M_∞=26`.
xmodel/nodal-realization-86-96-grok46-20260831.md:113:**Verdict: OPEN.** Ideal `I_25=(h_{23},h_{21},h_{19},h_{17},h_{15},h_{13},h_{11},h_9)` in the same ring, open `h_7≠0`, then `I_{DP}` of length 9, reduced, distinct tangents. Eight generators in ~9 variables: still finite, still CAS-scale. Same controls.
xmodel/nodal-realization-86-96-grok46-20260831.md:118:**Admissibility.** `Δ=(8,6,3)`: `2·3=6∈⟨8,6⟩`, `3<24`. `S_aff=⟨3,8⟩`, seven gaps, conductor 14. Germ `(2;29)`, `δ_∞=14`, `M_∞=30`. This is the Galindo-compatible replacement of the charged-review-refuted threat `(8,2,31)` (ROW-SWEEP and M-INF hostile review): `(8,6,1)` fails axiom (2) because `2·1∉⟨8,6⟩`.
xmodel/nodal-realization-86-96-grok46-20260831.md:129:I_29 = (h_23, h_21, h_19, h_17, h_15, h_13, h_11, h_9, h_7, h_5)
xmodel/nodal-realization-86-96-grok46-20260831.md:132:in the ring of §2, open `h_3≠0`, plus `I_{DP}` of length 7 reduced with distinct tangents. Ten odd generators, ~9 variables: overdetermined, msolve-scale; equivalently FSY Lemma 1 for `{6,8,3}`: write the second approximate root `g_2` of the monic-in-`y` defining polynomial from their Theorem 3 and impose `deg_t g_2(q(t),p(t))=3`, then saturate against `h_3=0` and the gcd-2 locus. Same positive/negative controls. If `I_29=⟨1⟩` (or every point of `V(I_29)` has non-reduced `I_{DP}`), the type is NON-REALIZABLE as a polynomial curve, hence as a nodal residual; if a nodal closed point exists it is REALIZED.
xmodel/nodal-realization-86-96-grok46-20260831.md:182:**AM / Galindo.** Every one of the six `Δ` satisfies Theorem 2.1 of Galindo–Monserrat (hashed in §0). Sathaye–Stenerson / Galindo converse realises each by a one-place curve of degree `d`; that is a local-plus-infinity existence theorem, not a genus-0 theorem, and not a nodal theorem (M-INF hostile review §3.2 already recorded this distinction for `(8,6,3)`). Semigroup genera match `δ_aff` and cannot kill a type that is already on the Galindo list. Primitive-generator uniqueness (FSY Lemma 3) distinguishes `δ`-sequences with the same semigroup (`⟨3,8⟩` as `{3,8}` versus `{8,6,3}`): it explains why `(t^3,t^8)` does not attain type `(8,6,3)`, but does not by itself forbid a different parametrization of degrees `(8,6)`.
